for me they're really just three  essential knives i use regularly today  i'll show you the basics on how to best  choose use and care for each one hey i'm  kelly a culinary producer at tasty and  these are my three favorite knives the  first knife i want to talk to you guys  about is the chef knife it's where i  really suggest you invest your money  since it's what you'll be using on a  daily basis there's two types of chef's  knife there's a western style and a  japanese style the western style has a  bellied blade which makes it easier for  the rocking motion when you're chopping  it also is a thicker steel which is  going to make it hold up more over time  but it is a little bit heavier in your  hand the second knife is a japanese  style knife as you can see it's a  straight edge blade which is better if  you like to do the chopping up and down  motion it is made of a lighter steel  which makes it sharper but it is prone  to chipping out of these two options i  prefer the western style knife because i  like to use the rocky motion when i chop  but at the end of the day i really want  you to choose what feels right for you  before you get to chopping the first  thing you need to do is make sure that  your knife is actually sharp you can  either take it to get professionally  sharpened you can use an electric  sharpener or you can use the stone like  i'm going to show you today this is a  sharpening stone there's two types of  grits there's a higher count and a lower  count you want to start at the lower  count to really get it sharp and then  you just want to get it really wet so  your knife will easily glide across the  stone and then you're going to hold it  at a 15 degree angle and pull it towards  you as you're sharpening you just want  to gently glide it across the stone so  you want to do this about 10 times per  side depending on how dull or sharp your  knife is then get it wet again  and do the other side  so now that i've sharpened it on the  core grit i'm just going to turn it over  and do it on the finer part this is like  gently just making our knife perfect so  after you've done that you just want to  wipe it off and clean off any grit  that's left over on your blade and  you're good to  go so in between sharpenings you want to  use this honing steel a lot of people  might think it's to sharpen your knife  but it's actually used to maintain your  knife it helps realign the blade's edge  in between sharpenings so what you want  to do is hold this steel horizontal and  then hold your knife in the other hand  in a vertical position and at a 15  degree angle just bring it down just  like we did with the sharpening and then  do the back side  now that our knife is sharp let's get to  cutting  so what you want to do is put your thumb  on one side of the blade and your index  finger on the other side then you're  going to wrap your three remaining  fingers around the handle and you'll  notice that you have a really good grip  you'll feel in control and it will  almost feel like an extension of your  arm so you do not want to have a flat  hand like this you will chop your  fingertip off what you want to do is  create a nice claw that will guide the  knife and keep your fingers safe  the first thing i'm going to show you  how to do is rough chop an onion its  name is exactly what it means we're just  going to roughly chop it we're not going  to be precise about it the first thing  i'm going to do is chop the top of the  onion off to create a flat surface then  you're going to cut it straight down the  middle from the root peel the onion skin  off  so now that i have one half of my onion  i'm going to take my knife and do about  one half inch slices going almost to the  root  then i'm going to take my knife and cut  horizontally across the center lastly  i'll cut across the top the other way to  create a rough chop and here's our rough  chop  the next thing i'm going to show you is  the dice this is a more precise smaller  cut that will help your food cook more  evenly so i'm going to dice a potato and  the first thing i want to do is create  an even surface for my cutting so i'm  going to cut the potato down the middle  and lay it flat on my board this way it  won't rock when i go to do the other  cuts then i'm going to take my knife  across the top and just create long  vertical slits trying to be as precise  as possible lay it flat again and do one  more cut down the middle and you can see  we have almost like a baton shape and  it's equal on all sides turn them again  and go back the other way creating about  one half inch pieces and if it's easier  just do a few at a time start small and  then you can perfect your cut this is  our dice so all the pieces are uniform  they're a little bit smaller than the  rough chop and they look great  the last cut i'm going to show you with  my chef's knife is the mince it is the  smallest type of cut you'll see in a  recipe so i'm going to show you how to  do that with garlic first you want to  take one clove off your bowl and use the  heel of your knife to press down  and that will help remove the skin  cut off the little stem and then you're  just going to take your knife and  finally rock back and forth one way turn  that pile another way and finally chop  the other way  and if that still doesn't feel small  enough for you just go back and forth  with your knife rocking back and forth  swiping off any garlic on the blade and  keep going  [Music]  so there you have it here are three cuts  with our chef knife we have a rough chop  a dice and a mince  my next favorite knife is the perry  knife it has a shorter blade than the  chef's knife and i love it because it's  great for those intricate knife cuts  that you might want to do so let me show  you three great ways to use it the grip  on a paring knife is going to be a  little bit different than a chef's knife  you're going to wrap four of your  fingers around the base and then put  your thumb right where the blade begins  you really want to hold it tight so you  have precise control  parry knives are great for coring fruits  and vegetables so for this jalapeno i'm  going to first slice the top off and  then use my knife to go around the edge  to remove that white pith i'm just going  to thinly slice it with my paring knife  so you can do this with your chef knife  but i think it's a little bit easier  when it's smaller so you have more  control so to remove the seats from a  jalapeno i'm just going to cut it  lengthwise to split in half  then i'm going to take the tip of my  paring knife and just go along that  white edge again just to remove all of  those little pieces and if you have to  go around twice don't worry about it and  look it comes off in one piece  and then we can just shake out all those  seeds all that spicy stuff  perrie knifes are also great for  removing the tops of strawberries so  you're going to take your strawberry in  one hand and your knife in the other and  just gently bring the knife across the  top using your thumbs as a base to hold  it in place and remove it and then if  you want to go in and remove the core  just bring it around the center pop it  out  so another great cut a paring knife can  do is segment your citrus it's a little  bit more difficult cut but i'll show you  how to make it easy the first step to  segment an orange is to cut off the top  and the bottom and this is again going  to create a sturdy base so the next step  is to take your paring knife at an angle  and just go along the side of the orange  following the pith to remove all the  white part so this type of cut is great  if you're going to use oranges in a  salad or as a garnish if you still have  a little bit of white don't worry about  it you can go back and clean it up with  your knife so all you're going to do is  take your paring knife at an angle and  kind of cut down in the v motion to  remove the segment so there you have it  a perfect little slice of orange  the next cut i'm going to show you with  a paring knife is a chiffonade and i'm  going to start with some basil i'm going  to take big leaves and stack them on top  of each other try to get all the same  size leaves and then roll them up from  the bottom to the top in a tight fashion  kind of like a cigar so once you have it  all rolled up tightly you're going to  take your knife and just starting from  the front and then going to the back cut  nice even slices and this is different  than the rocking motion you might do  with  other types of chopping you're gently  pulling it back so you don't bruise the  basil so this type of cut is great to  use as garnishes or even stirring into  pasta sauce right at the end so you get  tons of fresh basil flavor the perry  knife is a great tool to have in your  kitchen it is super versatile but let's  move on to the last knife i'm going to  talk about today  i love the serrated knife i feel like  it's a little bit underrated and i like  to think of it as a kitchen saw its  teeth on here make it super sharp and  great for cutting through tough  exteriors  one of the most common uses for a  serrated knife is cutting bread with it  it helps get through the hard exterior  without squishing the soft interior of  the bread you're going to hold a  serrated knife just like you would a  chef's knife make sure you get a really  good grip on it before you start cutting  so i have a baguette here and i'm going  to use my serrated knife to cut it on an  angle to make crostinis so i'm just  going back and forth just like a saw to  cut through the red and you can see it  held its shape without squishing the  interior so if i took a chef's knife to  try to do this first of all you can see  how much trouble i'm having  so you can see that this one is squished  and this one held its shape when i used  the serrated knife  serrated knifes are also great for  cutting through the tough skin of citrus  you don't want to use the chef's knife  on citrus because the acidity can  actually dull your blade so to cut this  citrus i'm actually going to cut really  thin rounds and use my serrated knife  going back and forth just like i did  with the bread to create nice even  slices we have these cute little rounds  that are evenly cut along all sides so  these lemon wheels are great for a  garnish for a cocktail or even putting  it on top of fish or chicken when baking  to add tons of flavor so another way to  cut citrus is in wedges and you would  use the serrated knife for that as well  so first i'm going to cut it in half and  then place the cut side down cut it in  half again  and then cut it in half one more time  and you have a cute little wedge  another cool way to use a serrated knife  is to level your cakes so when you  assemble them they're not lopsided  you're going to insert the knife halfway  through and then rotate the cake as  you're cutting in a sawing motion and  then you can take the top off and have a  nice level cake so this layer will stack  perfectly in a tiered cake  the last thing i want to talk to you  about is caring for your knives first  off make sure you wash and dry them  really well after each use the second is  invest in either a knife block or a  knife guard to keep them safe and sharp  so that about covers it i left a few  links below for my favorite type of  knives leave your questions in the  comments and thanks so much for watching  bye  oh yes  [Music]  you

